WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:04.500
DNA cloning refers to how we build
customized DNAs for various experiments

2
00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:08.730
in cellular and molecular biology, not
to be confused with the cloning of

3
00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:13.240
whole animals, like Dolly the sheep. We
often need to study a specific DNA,

4
00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:16.760
RNA, or protein, but the natural
version of it expressed in the cell

5
00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:21.230
needs to be tweaked a little bit so we
can study it efficiently. For example,

6
00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:25.780
we might want to add a florescent tag
to see a specific protein under the

7
00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:31.310
microscope. Remember the central dogma
in biology: DNA encodes RNA encodes

8
00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:35.420
protein. So whichever type of
macromolecule needs to be studied, it

9
00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:41.070
is originally expressed from DNA. That
is why we clone at the DNA level. For

10
00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:44.800
ease, we often work with small,
functional circular DNAs on the order

11
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:49.420
of 10,000 base pairs, called plasmids,
that express the DNA of interest.

12
00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:53.990
Plasmids can replicate and be
maintained in a cell and remain

13
00:00:53.990 --> 00:00:58.530
independent of the genome, and they
will continually express this protein

14
00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:04.069
of interest. Here's an example of a
classic cloning experiment. Remember

15
00:01:04.069 --> 00:01:09.600
what we want-- to overexpress a tagged
protein in the cell. We have a plasmid

16
00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:16.040
expressing the DNA for a protein, in
this example, the human histone. We

17
00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:20.550
want the histone to fluoresce for our
experiments. They usually don't, so we

18
00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:25.830
will add the DNA encoding GFP, which
stands for green fluorescent protein.

19
00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:28.890
We borrowed this DNA sequence from the
jellyfish.

20
00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:35.030
The original method to clone DNA used a
series of purified enzymes. First,

21
00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:41.220
molecular scissors, called restriction
enzymes, cut the plasmid. These enzymes

22
00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:47.250
recognize a very specific sequence of
DNA. Second, the GFP DNA is also cut

23
00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:49.550
with the same restriction enzyme.

24
00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:55.610
Third, we mix the two cut DNAs together.
Their ends have a special affinity for

25
00:01:55.610 --> 00:02:00.450
one another left behind by the
restriction enzymes. Fourth, an enzyme

26
00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:05.450
called ligase bonds the remaining gaps
in the DNAs to make a new plasmid with

27
00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:11.580
GFP fused to a histone. This plasmid
can then be introduced to human cells,

28
00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:16.170
allowing us to study a bright green
tagged human histone by fluorescence

29
00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:16.960
microscopy.

